<PUNCTUATION>==</PUNCTUATION> <HEADING2>Introduction</HEADING2>

This document demonstrates <BOLD>*AsciiDoc*</BOLD> syntax for highlighting.
AsciiDoc is a lightweight markup language for writing documentation.

Word wrap is especially useful for prose-heavy documents like this AsciiDoc file. When a paragraph extends well beyond the visible width of a typical terminal window, the editor should break lines at word boundaries so the full content is readable without horizontal scrolling. This makes editing documentation, technical manuals, and long-form content much more pleasant and efficient for writers and developers alike.

This second paragraph includes <BOLD>*bold text*</BOLD> and _italic text_ mixed with <FUNCTION>`inline monospaced code`</FUNCTION> to verify that syntax highlighting tokens remain correct when the editor wraps text across multiple visual rows. It also tests a <FUNCTION>https:</FUNCTION><TAG>//example.com</TAG><PUNCTUATION>[</PUNCTUATION><STRING>link to an example site</STRING><PUNCTUATION>]</PUNCTUATION> and references an <VARIABLE>{author}</VARIABLE> attribute to exercise various inline AsciiDoc elements within long wrapped content that should span at least three or four visual rows at eighty columns.

<PUNCTUATION>===</PUNCTUATION> <HEADING3>Basic Formatting</HEADING3>

Text can be <BOLD>*bold*</BOLD>, <ITALIC>_italic_</ITALIC>, <FUNCTION>`monospaced`</FUNCTION>, or [.underline]#underlined#.
You can also use <BOLD>**bold within**</BOLD> a word and <ITALIC>__italic within__</ITALIC> a word.

Superscript: E=mc^2^ and subscript: H~2~O.

